From 4a67f1b65ba3a852a246fd0b89379231fb8f0571 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 17:43:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/9] An agreement's terms are its own, asked about once, and
 read by its end, its commission and its submissions

Brief 27's Part 6 and loop 8 ask for scope, territory, medium and term reviewed and negotiated where supported. K3 said
the terms weren't negotiable because they were the unions' rules, but only the commission's cap is a rule; the term, the
scope and the territory were the game's words, and nothing read the scope or the territory: the pay kept its own copy of
them and the breakdowns read only the department's fields, so an agent put the actor up for a London feature the agreement
said it didn't cover. Each agreement now keeps its terms as versions (systems/representation.ts), the actor can ask once
while it's offered for a shorter term, voice-over or work abroad, the office answers two days on at a chance read from
how keen it is, and the version in force decides when the agreement ends, which pay carries commission, which offers the
agent handles and which parts the agent submits for. The cap stays where it was.

No agent covered voice work, so no office ever asked for a voice demo (Part 7, loop 7). Halberd Talent, the game's own
agency, gets a voice-over department and an invented agent in it, Celia Brannock: her office asks for and weighs the
demo as a legit office does the reel, her agreement covers voice-over, and voice casting takes an agent's submission
where an agreement covers voice. Real agents stay as their pages had them: no voice department is claimed for one, and
no real agent is met at a festival.

An introduction at a festival is a way in for the game's own agent (introduceAtFestival), for the festival's own system
to call once the player attends a screening and does its press; the approach reads it as it reads a referral.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
